# Line-ending policy for files whose BYTES are hashed and checked.
#
# The project default (core.autocrlf) rewrites line endings on checkout. For ordinary
# source that is harmless. For any file whose SHA-256 is recorded somewhere, it silently
# invalidates the recording on every fresh clone, and the failure looks like data
# corruption rather than like a checkout setting.
#
# Two different problems, two different attributes:
#
# `binary` (= -text -diff -merge) for preserved evidence. Those files genuinely contain
# CRLF, written by the runner. Pinning the bytes is required; suppressing the diff also
# stops `git diff --check` reading each preserved CR as trailing whitespace, which would
# otherwise fail on every line of frozen evidence. They are never hand-edited.
#
# This was a live bug, not a hypothetical: on a fresh Windows clone of v6.0.0,
# tests/test_web.py::test_verified_full_served_bytes_match_recorded_evidence_hashes
# failed, because artifacts/verified_full_report/analysis.json checked out as CRLF
# (8a950cad...) instead of the 12d47b35... recorded in its own export_meta.json. It
# passed only in the working copy where the report generator had written the file.
artifacts/** binary

# `-text` for generated LF artifacts whose hashes are recorded and asserted: the fixture
# pack indexes itself in MANIFEST.json, gate fixtures cite the real hashes of the frozen
# CI inputs, and the registry snapshot cites the registry. These stay readable and
# diffable -- they are LF already, so there is no CR for `git diff --check` to flag.
# Without this, four foundation tests fail on any fresh Windows clone.
tests/fixtures/stage3/** -text
analysis/case_sets/*.json -text
analysis/policies/*.json -text
analysis/remediation_rules.json -text
endpoint/targets.json -text

# Task 4 (OCES) frozen evidence. attacks/data/oces/freeze_content_hashes.json and
# freeze_provenance.json record a SHA-256 for each frozen artifact -- including the two
# generator sources, which is why .py files appear here. baseline/oces/build_oces.py
# regenerates that hash record from raw on-disk bytes, so without these entries a CRLF
# checkout both fails the freeze-integrity tests and silently rewrites the freeze record
# with environment-dependent hashes. The frozen content itself is unchanged and LF.
attacks/data/oces/** -text
baseline/oces/** -text

# Other Stage 3 data whose SHA-256 is quoted as identity (coverage map version and its
# historical overlay, the generated sentiment baseline and its provenance).
attacks/coverage_map.json -text
attacks/overlays/*.json -text
baseline/sentiment_baseline.json -text
baseline/sentiment_provenance.json -text

# Everything else keeps the project's existing text rules, unchanged.
